<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Falkirk (1298)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1298">1298</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-scottish-independence">First War of Scottish Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Falkirk was fought on 22 July 1298, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> defeated the Scottish army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a>. English longbowmen and cavalry broke the Scottish schiltrons, the dense formations of spearmen that had to stand still to resist mounted attack. Wallace resigned as Guardian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> after the defeat, though resistance continued.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge">Battle of Stirling Bridge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a> led a Scottish army south from November 1297 until January 1298, raiding the countryside from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>. Edward I made a truce with the French king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip the Fair</a> in October 1297 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 14 March 1298. He moved the centre of government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, where it stayed for six years, and declared the Scottish magnates traitors when none came to a council of war there. His army assembled at Roxburgh on 25 June and may have included about 2,000 armoured cavalry and some 12,000 paid infantry, though one estimate puts the infantry at only 8,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Edward was about to fall back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> when he learned that the Scots were at Torwood, near Falkirk. On 22 July he placed his army south of the town. The Scots were chiefly spearmen drawn up in four great schiltrons, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> between them and about 500 mounted knights at the rear. The Comyns and Robert Bruce were absent, and Andrew Moray, who had shared the victory at Stirling Bridge, had died of wounds from that battle.</p><p>The English cavalry advanced in four divisions. They routed the Scottish cavalry and overran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> under Sir John Stewart, but the schiltrons held, and 111 English horses were killed against the spears. Edward's archers, crossbowmen and slingers then shot into the packed, poorly armoured spearmen, who could neither retreat nor attack. When the ranks were thin and disordered, the English cavalry and infantry broke the schiltrons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Scottish leaders were not lost in great numbers, but the dead included Wallace's second-in-command Sir John de Graham, Sir John Stewart and Macduff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>. Wallace resigned as Guardian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> shortly afterwards. Edward occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> and raided Perth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a>, but was back at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> by 9 September, and he invaded again in the summer of 1300. The historian Stuart Reid judged that Wallace, a good partisan leader, was not suited to leading a conventional army. The exact site of the battle is uncertain, with three proposed locations. The Falkirk Roll, a list of the arms of English bannerets present, is the oldest known English occasional roll of arms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Falkirk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Wallace defeated an English army at the Battle of Stirling Bridge in 1297 but lost at <b>Falkirk</b> in 1298.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
